    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\nToyota China Recalls Land Cruiser Prado Hybrid and Lexus GX 550h for Potential Fuel Leak\n49 photos\nPhoto: Toyota\nPer the State Administration for Market Regulation, the world's largest automaker has to fix a bunch of body-on-frame sport utility vehicles in China. This recall affects 47,347 hybrid units of the domestically produced Prado (a.k.a. Land Cruiser in the United States or 250 series if you're into Land Cruisers) and 8,130 imported examples of the mechanically similar Lexus GX 550h.\nAs it happens, the problem with these body-on-frame SUVs can be found where customers rarely look. The retainer inside the fuel link quick connector may lift due to unexpected external forces, and in extreme cases, detachment could occur. This, in turn, creates the possibility of a catastrophic fuel leak.\nToyota's Chinese subsidiary has not specified which road conditions or driving maneuvers trigger the problem. The filing published by the SAMR only describes the circumstances in general, and that would be that, unfortunately. FAW Toyota Motor is the legal entity behind recall S2026M0133I, which covers the four-pot turbo hybrid version of the 250-series Land Cruiser Prado.\nProduction dates range from July 15, 2024, through October 6, 2026. So yeah, the problem has only recently been contained in vehicle production. Toyota Motor (China) Investment handles the Lexus side of the biz. Its recall, which is listed as S2026M0134I, includes GX 550h vehicles manufactured in Japan between February 28, 2024, and October 1, 2026.\nWhile they may be handled as separate campaigns, the problem is shared. Furthermore, the manufacturing windows also overlap considerably. Regarding the remedy, service technicians first have to inspect the quick-connect fittings. If no abnormalities are found, Toyota and Lexus techs are to install an additional fixing cover designed to prevent a separation.\nPhoto: Toyota Vehicles that seem a bit off, meanwhile, will receive a brand-new fuel line assembly and the fixing cover as well. Naturally, these remedies come at no charge to affected customers in the People's Republic of China. The SAMR filing also reveals that customers will be contacted through registered mail and other channels. Alternatively, customers may contact Toyota and Lexus by phone.\nAdditional details are available via the State Administration for Market Regulation Recall Center or on the SAMRDPRC WeChat account. Last but certainly not least, neither brand has announced a deadline for completing the inspections. The campaigns, however, take effect immediately.\nThe China-built model is advertised with 326 ponies at full throttle. Equipped with a NiMH battery, an eight-speed automatic tranny, and full-time AWD with a locking center differential, the Prado for the PRC outputs 630 Newton-meters (465 pound-feet) of torque. Said components and figures are shared with the GX 550h, which is understandably pricier due to its badge and more generous standard kit.\n778,000 yuan is the starting price of the Pioneer grade, whereas 899,800 yuan gets you the Lingfeng ULTRA trim level. At current exchange rates, those prices can be converted to 116,320 and 134,530 dollars.\nVia // SAMR\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nToyota toyota land cruiser Lexus Lexus GX Hybrid China recall\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Don’t Miss This Pristine Dodge Challenger Hemi Scat Pack Shaker, It’s a Single-Owner Muscle Car",
